            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "> is `poke_eventloop` actually needed? generally shouldn't be needed, because expect() will wait until the ESC is processed\r\n\r\nIt seems so. I ran a few test runs:\r\n\r\n```\r\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────┐\r\n│                              config                              │ failures │\r\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────┤\r\n│ without poke_eventloop, idle machine                             │ 0/100    │\r\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────┤\r\n│ without poke_eventloop, under CPU load (24 spinners on 16 cores) │ 13/100   │\r\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────┤\r\n│ with poke_eventloop, under the same load                         │ 0/100    │\r\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\nI don't understand it to explain why myself but here's AI's reasoning:\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>AI Generated Explanation</summary>\r\n\r\nThe concern isn't whether ESC gets processed — it's the ordering between ESC and the deferred accept.\r\n\r\nget() doesn't apply anything synchronously; it defers via vim.schedule() (inline_completion.lua:477), and the scheduled callback reads completor.current. <Esc> leaves insert mode, which aborts the completor and clears current. If ESC wins the race, the callback finds nil and returns — the buffer is never modified, so screen:expect() retries against a state that will never arrive.\r\n\r\nAnd nvim prioritizes available input over queued events, so that race is losable:\r\n\r\n- state.c:59-65 — if (vpeekc() != NUL || typebuf.tb_len > 0) key = safe_vgetc(); … else if (!multiqueue_empty(main_loop.events)) key = K_EVENT;\r\n- state.c:126-131 — state_handle_k_event() bails out of the drain loop as soon as input_available()\r\n\r\nThis is the same thing da78772328 (\"test(lsp): fix flakiness in inline completion test\", #35676) fixed for get() applies the current candidate, which is where I copied the idiom from:\r\n\r\n▎ The flakiness happens because get() uses vim.schedule(), and a following key may be processed before the scheduled event. Use poke_eventloop() to ensure that the scheduled event is processed.\r\n\r\n</details>",
            "commit_id": "6bd445f52f08821d96842450fb51088d65a44be1",
            "created_at": "2026-08-24T12:01:28Z",
            "diff_hunk": "@@ -280,6 +280,19 @@ describe('vim.lsp.inline_completion', function()\n         },\n       }, result)\n     end)\n+\n+    it('accepts an item after the line shrank past its range', function()\n+      feed('i')\n+      screen:expect({ grid = grid_with_candidates })\n+      exec_lua(function()\n+        -- Shrink the line the item's range covers, as backspacing would.\n+        vim.api.nvim_buf_set_text(0, 0, 18, 0, 20, {})\n+        vim.lsp.inline_completion.get()\n+      end)\n+      n.poke_eventloop()\n+      feed('<Esc>')\n+      screen:expect({ grid = grid_applied_candidates })",
